{"name":"Green Hornet","hex":"#6a9d5d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Green Hornet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In the Vietnam War, a member of the US Air Force's 20th Special Operations Squadron, who used the Bell UH-1F variant of the \"Huey\" military helicopter.","A cocktail consisting of equal parts brandy, sake, and melon liqueur."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Green_Hornet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green Hornet","blocks":[{"text":"The Green Hornet is a superhero created in 1936 by George W. Trendle and Fran Striker, with input from radio director James Jewell."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Van_Williams_as_Green_Hornet_1966_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Hornet","wikidata":"Q1510225"},{"title":"Hornet","blocks":[{"text":"Hornets are wasps of the genus Vespa in the subfamily Vespinae. They are the largest of the eusocial wasps. Species like the European hornet can grow to 35 mm (1.4 in) in length, while other species like the Asian giant hornet can exceed 50 mm (2.0 in). They are similar in appearance to their close relatives, the yellowjackets, but are distinguished from other vespine wasps by the relatively large top margin of the head, or vertex. Worldwide, 22 species of Vespa are recognized. Most species only occur in the tropics of Asia, though the European hornet is widely distributed throughout Europe, Russia, North America, and north-eastern Asia. Wasps of the genus Dolichovespula native to North America are commonly called hornets, but are actually yellowjackets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Hornet.jpg/330px-Hornet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornet","wikidata":"Q334422"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Green_Hornet_Comics_31.jpg/500px-Green_Hornet_Comics_31.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Green_Hornet_Comics_31.jpg/500px-Green_Hornet_Comics_31.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Green Hornet Comics #31 (November-December 1946), art by Al Avison","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Hornet_Comics_31.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg/500px-Vespa_crabro_germana_with_prey_Richard_Bartz_Crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikim